<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">William, Prince of Albania</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1876">1876</a>–<a href="/years/1945">1945</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Wilhelm of Wied, Prince Wilhelm of Wied</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">William, Prince of Albania, born Prince Wilhelm of Wied, was a German officer chosen by the Great Powers in 1913 as ruler of the new Principality of Albania. He arrived at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durres">Durrës</a> in March 1914 but faced rebellion in central Albania, rivalry with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/essad-pasha-toptani">Essad Pasha Toptani</a> and the outbreak of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, and left the country in September 1914 after six months, without formally abdicating.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/william-prince-of-albania.jpg" alt="William, Prince of Albania" width="320" height="474" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Princ_Vidi_(portret).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Wilhelm Friedrich Heinrich, Prince of Wied, was born on 26 March 1876 at Neuwied Castle, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koblenz">Koblenz</a>, in the Prussian Rhineland. He belonged to the mediatised house of Wied-Neuwied and was the third son of William, 5th Prince of Wied, brother of Queen Elisabeth of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>, and Princess Marie of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, sister of Queen Louise of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>. He served as a Prussian cavalry officer and became a captain in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-general-staff">German General Staff</a> in 1911.</p><p>Albania declared independence from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> in November 1912, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-balkan-war">First Balkan War</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-london-1915">Treaty of London</a> recognised it in 1913. The six powers selected him, and the announcement was made in November 1913. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelm-ii">Wilhelm II</a> thought the choice unwise.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>On 7 February 1914 William told the Great Powers he would accept the throne, and on 21 February a delegation of Albanian notables led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/essad-pasha-toptani">Essad Pasha Toptani</a> formally asked him. He arrived at the provisional capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durres">Durrës</a> on 7 March and formed a cabinet dominated by aristocrats, with Essad Pasha as minister of defence and foreign affairs. He was styled sovereign prince abroad, but in Albania he was called mbret, or king.</p><p>His reign was turbulent. Revolts of Muslims broke out in central Albania against Essad Pasha and against foreign domination that was not Turkish, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> encouraged a separatist provisional government in North Epirus. Essad Pasha accepted money from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> to finance a revolt and a coup. The plot was exposed, and he was arrested on 19 May 1914, tried for treason and sentenced to death, but the Italian government saved his life and he went into exile in Italy. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> began, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a> demanded Albanian soldiers; he refused, citing Albania's neutrality in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-london-1915">Treaty of London</a>, and his remuneration was cut off.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>William left Albania on 3 September 1914, heading for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, and insisted that he remained head of state. He returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and rejoined the army. His hopes of restoration rose in early 1916 and came to nothing, and after the war the Paris Peace Conference was unlikely to restore the throne to someone who had fought against its participants.</p><p>Several factions in post-war Albania billed themselves as regencies for him, but the country was declared a republic on 31 January 1925, officially ending his reign. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zog-i">Ahmet Zogu</a> was set to become king, William reaffirmed his claim for himself and his heirs. He died at Predeal, near Sinaia, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>, and was buried at the Lutheran Church in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bucharest">Bucharest</a>, leaving his son Carol Victor as heir to his Albanian claims.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Wilhelm, Prince of Albania” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Conference of Ambassadors in London made Albania an independent principality under the Great Powers' guarantee and chose the German <b>Prince Wilhelm of Wied</b> as ruler.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-albania">Principality of Albania</a></li><li><blockquote>In March 1914 the Great Powers' chosen ruler, <b>Wilhelm of Wied</b>, arrived to found the Principality of Albania.</blockquote><a href="/state/provisional-government-of-albania">Provisional Government of Albania</a></li><li><blockquote>Essad accepted the authority of <b>Wilhelm of Wied</b> in early 1914, becoming minister of the interior and war in the Principality of Albania, but was expelled for intrigue in May 1914.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-central-albania">Republic of Central Albania</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neuwied">Neuwied</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in Predeal, in what is now <a href="/countries/romania">Romania</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.43,7.46&amp;year=1920" aria-label="Neuwied on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4720 412 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of William, Prince of Albania"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5170 682h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5170 682h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.43,7.46&amp;year=1920">What was here in 1920? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
